  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "A random sample of 5 parts is taken from each of machines A and B. Their lengths (mm) are: A: 100, 100, 101, 99, 100; B: 97, 100, 101, 101, 101 (both have a mean of 100). How many times A's sample variance is B's sample variance?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "6",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Under the recorded interpretation, the requested quotient is B's sample variance divided by A's. Both means are \\(100\\), so A's variance is \\((0+0+1+1+0)\\div5=2/5\\), and B's variance is \\((9+0+1+1+1)\\div5=12/5\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{B's variance}}{\\text{A's variance}}=\\frac{12/5}{2/5}=6.\n\\]\nB's sample has greater variation. However, the displayed wording can also be read as asking for A's variance divided by B's, which gives \\((2/5)\\div(12/5)=1/6\\). Thus the stored key \\(6\\) applies to the recorded B/A interpretation; the reciprocal A/B reading gives \\(1/6\\).",
